Participants observed that inflation had picked up over the intermeeting period, driven in part by a sharp rise in energy prices and persistent increases in shelter costs. Core measures also moved higher, and several participants viewed the upside surprises as broad based.

Wage growth remained strong, and many participants reported that firms in their Districts continued to raise compensation to retain workers. Some contacts indicated plans to pass higher labor costs on to customers, which raised concerns about a wage-price dynamic.

Survey measures of near-term inflation expectations rose noticeably, while longer-term expectations remained broadly stable. Participants warned that a sustained increase in inflation expectations could make it harder to return inflation to two percent.

Nearly all participants supported raising the target range for the federal funds rate by 25 basis points at this meeting. They judged that a tighter policy stance was needed to ensure inflation returned to the Committee's objective and that additional increases might be warranted.

One participant preferred to hold the target range steady, citing the lagged effects of earlier tightening and signs of slowing demand in interest-sensitive sectors. That participant noted that a rate hike risked overshooting.

Participants also discussed the risk that energy supply disruptions could keep headline inflation elevated, and they emphasized readiness to adjust the stance of policy as warranted by incoming data.
